    <!-- ================================ Electrical ================================ -->
    <string name="step_elec_shock_first_action_prompt">A workmate is being shocked and cannot let go. What do you do first?</string>
    <string name="step_elec_shock_first_action_remedy">Isolate the supply first. Touching them while the current is on makes two casualties.</string>
    <string name="step_elec_cable_damage_prompt">Point at what must not be touched.</string>
    <string name="step_elec_cable_damage_remedy">The damaged cable. Treat any exposed conductor as live until it has been proved dead.</string>
    <string name="step_first_response_sequence_prompt">The supply is off and they are unconscious. Put your actions in order.</string>
    <string name="step_first_response_sequence_remedy">Danger, response, shout for help, airway, breathing, compressions. Checking for danger first is what keeps you alive.</string>
    <string name="step_elec_permit_to_work_prompt">What do you need before working on an electrical panel?</string>
    <string name="step_elec_permit_to_work_remedy">A permit and a proved isolation. Low voltage is not the same as safe.</string>
    <string name="step_burn_first_aid_prompt">A workmate has an electrical burn on their forearm. What do you do?</string>
    <string name="step_burn_first_aid_remedy">Cool it with clean running water for twenty minutes. No ice, no oil, no paste, do not burst blisters.</string>

